# Design Binding — figma

`DESIGN.md` `## Screens × form factors` lists component **names** only. The per-element detail
— text labels, badges, hint text, timestamp **format**, app-shell chrome, control order — lives in
the Figma frame the table links to. Implementing from the name list alone drops elements silently.
This skill closes that gap.

The agent has already read `DESIGN.md`. For every screen a UI task implements (plan time) or has
implemented (implement time), run the three steps below. Read-only on Figma — never mutate the file.

## Step 1 — Locate the artifact

In `DESIGN.md § Screens × form factors`, each screen row links to a Figma frame (file URL +
node id). Two access paths, depending on the calling agent's tools:

- **Caller has `mcp__figma__*`** (e.g. a design-review agent): fetch the frame's node tree via the
  Figma MCP. Full element diff possible (Step 2, MCP path).
- **Caller lacks Figma MCP** (the default impl-planning-agent / implementation-agent — their
  `tools:` list is Read/Write/Edit/Bash/Grep/Glob, no MCP): you cannot read the live frame. Use
  `DESIGN.md`'s per-screen element detail instead (Step 2, DESIGN.md path), and raise the
  not-auto-verified flag in Step 3.

## Step 2 — Build the element manifest

**MCP path** — walk the frame's node tree: TEXT nodes (copy), component INSTANCE nodes (named
atoms/molecules), auto-layout children (order is meaningful). Skip Figma-only scaffolding
(annotation frames, redline layers, the design-system page).

**DESIGN.md path** — read every per-screen element detail DESIGN.md carries (the authoring
`figma-design` skill records component instances and state templates). This is necessarily coarser
than the live frame — flag accordingly in Step 3.

Either path produces an ordered list per screen of every user-visible element:

- text labels + exact copy · badges / chips · hint / helper / error text
- timestamps **and their format** (relative vs absolute) · icons + accessible labels
- sub-components nested in a row / card — including a child a later feature added to an
  earlier-feature component (e.g. a badge on an existing list row)
- app-shell chrome: header, page title, nav, toolbar buttons + labels
- control order within a group (e.g. Save before Cancel)

## Step 3 — Diff against the code + flag

For each manifest element, confirm the implemented component renders it (or, at plan time, that a
task covers it). "Renders it" means render evidence, not source-code presence: a name-grep over
templates/source is necessary but NOT sufficient — every manifest element can be "present in code"
while the app renders blank. When the app can run and browser-capable tooling exists, verify
against the running screen (runner snapshot / screenshot). When render verification isn't possible
this session, the diff outcome for that screen is a flag (`visual fidelity not render-verified —
confirm by eye at /arh-human-review`), never a PASS.

For any element present but absent from the code **and** not in the task scope /
PLAN.md file plan, append to `docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/FLAGS.md` (create if absent):

```
### AF-<next>: inconsistency · task: T-NN · <code-file>
design element "<element>" on screen <S> present in Figma frame but not implemented — DESIGN.md component list omitted it.
```

**DESIGN.md path** (caller lacked Figma MCP): additionally raise ONE flag per screen

```
### AF-<next>: risky-pattern · task: T-NN · docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/DESIGN.md
visual fidelity for screen <S> not auto-verified — implementation agent has no Figma MCP access; diffed against DESIGN.md detail only, which may under-specify elements. Confirm by eye at /arh-human-review.
```

`<next>` is the next sequential AF id. Triaged at `/arh-human-review` before commit-PR. Never silently
skip an element because the component "looks done."

## Contract (uniform across all design providers)

- **Input:** `DESIGN.md` (already read) + the Figma frame (or DESIGN.md detail when MCP-less).
- **Output:** zero or more `AF-NN` flags; the UI verified element-complete against the artifact.
- **Read-only** on the design source. Never weaken: a missing element is always a flag.

